<Layout title="About — Vivid Savitri-Hampton" pathname="/about/">
  <article class="container-wide pt-8 pb-24">
    <p class="small-caps mb-8">About</p>

    <h1 class="mb-8">
      Player-coach product builder, working at the intersection of frontier AI and human-centred design.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose-body">
      <p>
        I'm Vivid. I build AI systems that are useful enough to ship and careful
        enough to defend. Based in Sydney, working across product strategy, research
        prototyping, and engineering.
      </p>

      <p>
        Background spans HCI and design (Stanford), AI product management (Duke), an MBA
        thesis on AI-driven experience design (Berlin), and twelve years across twelve
        countries — most relevantly two successful exits in the video games space (Shanghai
        and Berlin) where I learned how to ship products with small teams under deadline.
      </p>

      <h2>What I work on</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Agentic AI systems</strong> — multi-agent architectures with explicit
          safety envelopes, LLM orchestration, evaluation harnesses
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>MCP servers</strong> — production and prototype, both consumer-facing
          and inside the enterprise (Breville's DAM Butler is the most public example)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Privacy-first AI infrastructure</strong> — provenance-aware retrieval,
          longitudinal reasoning scaffolds, human-in-the-loop review gates
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Human-AI interaction</strong> — designing experiences where AI augments
          decision-making instead of obscuring it
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why I care about safety</h2>

      <p>
        Most AI demos look good on happy-path prompts and fall apart when you ask the
        hard questions — provenance, override, longitudinal change, failure modes. My
        bet is that the products that matter five years from now are the ones whose
        builders took those questions seriously at the prototype stage, not after the
        first incident.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's what threads my work together: <a href="/projects/vai-sante-os/">multimodal
        memory with chain-of-custody</a>, <a href="/projects/vivid-alpaca/">multi-agent
        trading with execution guardrails</a>, <a href="/projects/espresso-horoscope-mcp/">local
        agents with strict tool boundaries</a>. The use cases differ. The discipline doesn't.
      </p>

      <h2>Currently building</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          AI-native gameplay with agentic workflows and isolated sub-agents for parallel
          task execution
        </li>
        <li>
          Hybrid model routing across local models (LM Studio) and OpenRouter-hosted
          models, including Qwen 3.x variants
        </li>
        <li>
          AI evaluation frameworks aimed at product decisions, not benchmark gaming
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Education</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>AI Product Management — Duke University (Pratt School of Engineering)</li>
        <li>MBA — Steinbeis Hochschule, Berlin (Thesis: AI-Driven Application for Experience Design)</li>
        <li>HCI & Design — Stanford University</li>
      </ul>

